# db/migrations

Alembic migrations for the Postgres schema in `apx/db/models.py` (db/migrations).
`env.py` resolves the connection URL from `DATABASE_URL` (falling back to
`alembic.ini`'s `sqlalchemy.url`) and runs migrations through an `asyncpg` engine.

## Every schema change needs a new revision with explicit DDL

Revision `0001_initial_schema` builds its tables by calling
`Base.metadata.create_all(bind)` directly against the live ORM models, rather than emitting
frozen `CREATE TABLE` statements. That was a one-time shortcut for the first migration only:
because it re-reads `apx.db.models` at run time, `0001` alone cannot drift from the models
package, and a *brand-new* database looks identical whether it was created by
`Base.metadata.create_all()` directly or by `alembic upgrade head`.

That guarantee stops at revision `0001`. Any database that has already run `0001` (every
non-fresh dev, staging, or production database) only changes when a *new* Alembic revision
adds explicit `op.add_column(...)` / `op.alter_column(...)` / `op.execute(...)` DDL for the
change. If a future PR edits `apx/db/models.py` (adds a column, changes a type, adds a
constraint) without also adding a revision under `db/migrations/versions/` that applies the
same change with real DDL, the ORM models and any already-migrated database will silently
diverge: a fresh `create_all()` database matches the new models, an `alembic upgrade head`
database on top of `0001` does not, and nothing fails loudly until a query hits the missing
column or constraint in a deployed environment.

**Rule:** every change to a mapped table in `apx/db/models.py` ships with a new
`db/migrations/versions/000N_*.py` revision in the same PR, with explicit `upgrade()` /
`downgrade()` DDL (autogenerate is a fine starting point; review the output, it does not
reliably capture computed columns, `CHECK` constraints, or triggers). `0001` is the only
revision allowed to take the `Base.metadata.create_all()` shortcut.

`tests/integration/test_migrations.py::test_models_have_no_undeclared_migration_drift`
guards this: it runs `alembic upgrade head` against a throwaway Postgres container and diffs
the result against `apx/db/models.py` with Alembic's own autogenerate comparison, failing if
a model change has no corresponding migration DDL.
